4.3 Reduction from #HAM to #FMO
The #FMO problem is clearly in # P, since we can take a solution string x as a certiﬁcate. Therefore,
we focus on its completeness.
We are given an undirected graph G = ⟨V, E ⟩, along with its source w and its destination s.
The reduction goes as follows.
• Build an instance ⟨R, F ⟩ as described in Section 4.1.
• Let z be the number of solutions for the instance ⟨R, F ⟩.
• Let a = ∏ |V |
i=1 α i ⁄= 0, where α i is deﬁned as follows for a vertex i of degree di:
α i =
{ 2(di−1) (di − 1)! i = w, s
2(di−2) (di − 2)! i ⁄= w, s
• Return the integer z/a .
The above reduction takes polynomial time. To see its correc tness, it suﬃces to show that
|Σ H| = a for every Hamiltonian path H = ⟨i1, i 2, . . . , i |V |⟩ in G.
15
