+Step-0 audit: Section 1.2 gives the easy result that ordinary set C1P is solved
+and represented by Booth--Lueker PQ-trees in linear time, whereas Theorem 16
+and Corollary 17 make counting and deciding the multiset version #P-complete
+and NP-complete.  The Section 4 proof obtains a witness from a Hamiltonian path,
+but at the <=256-atom answer sizes its planted sparse graphs were repeatedly
+solved by direct Hamiltonian DFS, so that attempted Track-A distribution was
+discarded.  The shipped distribution is explicitly Track B: enumerate all
+modular steps, cube their residues, and check the resulting doubled cycles.
+That exact reference algorithm is polynomial and its measured work is reported.
+
+Generation first selects the answer.  For prime p == 2 (mod 3), cubing is a
+permutation of the residues.  The planted word consists of two copies of the
+cycle (j*d)^3 mod p.  Each required Q has one copy of every residue plus a
+second copy of three consecutive cycle symbols, so Q is literally the
+multiset of a length-(p+3) planted substring.  All requirements are sampled
+from the same three-arc distribution and shuffled.
+
+The outlier attack sorts symbols by constraint frequency; the numeric attack
+tries the visible residue order; the co-occurrence greedy walk follows local
+frequency only; the overlap walk joins triples without backtracking across
+missing windows; the cube-root-sorted probe notices the power map but not its
+step; and 256 uniform restarts sample the stated multiset-permutation language.
+The successful modular-step enumerator is kept out of attacks, as Track B
+requires, and is reported separately as reference_algorithm.
