The paper’s key distinction is already clear: ordinary set-C1P is solvable in linear time with PQ-trees, while the multiset decision problem is NP-complete via the paper’s Section 4 Hamiltonian-path reduction. I’m now checking whether inverse “random intervals around a planted ordering” would actually inherit that hard regime—it usually does not—and whether the reduction itself gives a usable theorem-backed generator.
